---

<Layout
  title="Study Desk help | Liberation and Inclusion Translation"
  description="Help for the Study Desk preview: the desk page and its panes, sheets, the Notebook, where your notes are kept, saving and opening a copy, and what each part does."
  canonical="https://litbible.net/desk/help/"
  robotsNoindex={true}
>
  <article class="nbhelp" aria-labelledby="nbhelp-title">
    <div class="nbhelp__layout">

      {/* The title first, then the contents, then the text: a phone and a
          screen reader meet the page's name before its list. On a wide
          window the grid puts the contents beside both (desk-help.css). */}
      <header class="nbhelp__header">
        <h1 id="nbhelp-title" class="nbhelp__title">Study Desk help</h1>
        <p class="nbhelp__lede">
          The Study Desk is a preview of study tools for reading on a
          computer. It is switched on for a few readers while it is tried
          out. This page explains it, and you can read it whether or not it
          is on for you.
        </p>
      </header>

      <nav class="nbhelp__toc" aria-labelledby="contents">
        <h2 id="contents" class="nbhelp__toc-title">On this page</h2>
        <ol class="nbhelp__toc-parts">
          {parts.map((part) => (
            <li>
              <a class="nbhelp__toc-part" href={`#${part.id}`}>{part.title}</a>
              {part.topics.length > 0 && (
                <ul class="nbhelp__toc-topics">
                  {part.topics.map((topic) => (
                    <li><a href={`#${topic.id}`}>{topic.title}</a></li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </nav>

        <div class="nbhelp__body">

          {/* ============ 1. Getting started ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="start">
            <h2 id="start">Getting started</h2>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="what-it-is">
              <h3 id="what-it-is">What the Study Desk is</h3>
              <p>The Study Desk has four parts:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>A notebook</strong> that holds your notes, highlights,
                  bookmarks, and pinned footnotes, and stays with you as you
                  read.</li>
                <li><strong>The Study Desk page</strong>, where panes sit side by
                  side: the text, your notes, the footnotes, the Greek, and
                  more.</li>
                <li><strong>Sheets</strong>, handouts you can print, present, or
                  share.</li>
                <li><strong>The Greek text</strong>, with each word’s details
                  and a lexicon.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Everything you keep stays in your browser, on your computer.
                Nothing is sent to the LIT Bible team or anywhere else, so
                <a href="#copies">save a copy</a> now and then. The Study Desk
                is for computers.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="where-things-are">
              <h3 id="where-things-are">Where to find each part</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>On a Scripture page</strong> (one chapter) and in
                  Read View, <a href="#panel">the Notebook</a> sits beside the
                  text, and the verse menu and the selection panel have a “My
                  Notebook” group.</li>
                <li><strong>The header’s “Study” menu</strong> lists the
                  <a href="/desk/">Study Desk</a>, <a href="/sheets/">My
                  sheets</a>, <a href="/notebook/">My Notebook</a> (everything
                  you have kept, on one page), and this help.</li>
                <li><strong>“Open in Study Desk”</strong> in a Scripture page’s
                  toolbar opens the <a href="#desk-page">Study Desk page</a> at
                  that chapter.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="tour">
              <h3 id="tour">Take a tour</h3>
              <p>
                There are three tours. Each shows the thing working, opening a
                menu or a tab for you. You can skip a part or the whole tour.
                None starts by itself, none keeps anything, and when one ends
                the page is as you left it.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>The notebook tour</strong> walks through the notebook
                  on a page of scripture, in three parts: marking the text, the
                  Notebook, and finding and keeping.
                  <a href="/john-3/?desk-tour">Take the notebook tour</a> on
                  John 3. It needs the Study Desk preview to be on for you.</li>
                <li><strong>The Study Desk tour</strong> runs on the Study Desk
                  page, in two parts: what is on the desk, then how to change
                  it. It shows the original layout while it runs, adds a pane,
                  shows a Sheets pane and the template chooser, and tries a
                  layout, and it puts your own layout back when it ends. The
                  first time the Study Desk opens it offers the tour, and after
                  that the page’s title menu has “Take the tour.”
                  <a href="/desk/?desk-tour">Take the Study Desk tour</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>The Sheets tour</strong> runs on the Study Desk page,
                  in a Sheets pane, and on the Sheets page, in two parts:
                  making a sheet (the list, the templates, the parts, a passage
                  and its blanks, your own words) and using one (printing,
                  sharing, presenting). The sheet it works on is the tour’s own
                  and is never saved. The first time a Sheets pane or the
                  Sheets page opens it offers the tour, and each page’s list of
                  sheets has “Take the Sheets tour.”
                  <a href="/desk/?sheet-tour">Take it in the Study Desk</a> or
                  <a href="/sheets/?sheet-tour">on the Sheets page</a>.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 2. Marking what you read ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="marking">
            <h2 id="marking">Marking what you read</h2>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="making-marks">
              <h3 id="making-marks">Notes, highlights, and bookmarks</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>To mark a verse</strong>, select its number on a
                Scripture page. The menu that opens has a “My Notebook” group:
                “Highlight,” “Add a note,” “Add bookmark,” “Add to sheet,”
                “Print,” and “Show in Notebook.”
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>To mark only some words</strong>, select them instead.
                The same choices appear beside the selection.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>A <strong>note</strong> appears in the margin beside its
                  words.</li>
                <li>A <strong>highlight</strong> tints them.</li>
                <li>A <strong>bookmark</strong> shows beside the verse, in gold
                  if it stays put and in red if it moves as you read (see
                  <a href="#bookmarks">Bookmarks that keep your place</a>).</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                In Read View you can bookmark and highlight (“Highlight” shows
                there while “My highlights” is on in the Display settings), but
                notes belong to Scripture pages. Notes go on a verse or on words you select,
                not on a footnote.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>To take one away</strong>, find it in “My Notes” and
                delete it. Deleting can be undone with Ctrl+Z (⌘Z on a Mac), or
                with the Undo button that appears for a few seconds. Ctrl+Y
                (⇧⌘Z on a Mac) does it again. What you delete waits in
                <a href="#delete-everything">the trash</a>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="bookmarks">
              <h3 id="bookmarks">Bookmarks that keep your place</h3>
              <p>
                A bookmark shows beside a verse: a spot you want to return to.
                There are two kinds, and a verse can hold one of each.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>One that stays put</strong> is gold. It stays on its
                  verse until you remove it.</li>
                <li><strong>One that moves as you read</strong> is red. In the
                  Notebook, “Edit” gives a bookmark a name, such as “Thursday
                  group,” and can turn on “Move as I read.” Open it from your
                  list and read on, and it follows you, so it always shows
                  where the group left off. Up to {MAX_FOLLOWING} bookmarks can
                  move this way.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                While a bookmark follows you, its mark is an outline, because
                it is only resting where you are. Reading on to the next
                chapter carries it along. Looking something up, such as a note
                in another chapter, pauses it where you left off, and a bar
                offers to move it to where you are.
              </p>
              <p>
                “Continue reading” is not a bookmark. It is the one place that
                is always there, at the top of your list: where you stopped
                last.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="labels">
              <h3 id="labels">Labels</h3>
              <p>
                Labels are your own words for sorting what you keep. Give a
                note, highlight, or bookmark a label, and filter by it in the
                Notebook and in My Notebook. They belong to you; they are not
                the topics the site uses for its own chapters.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="footnote-pins">
              <h3 id="footnote-pins">Pinned footnotes</h3>
              <p>
                Beside each footnote is a small thumbtack to pin it (“Pin
                footnote 3”). It is filled and tinted green while the footnote
                is pinned, and pressing it again unpins it.
              </p>
              <p>
                A pinned footnote is kept under “Pinned footnotes” in “My
                Notes,” with its reference and the footnote as it read when you
                pinned it. If the LIT later revises that footnote, the pin says
                so.
              </p>
              <p>Where the thumbtack is:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>In a chapter’s Footnotes list</strong>, it sits in
                  the left margin beside the footnote when there is room, and
                  stays out of sight until you point at a footnote or reach it
                  with Tab. A pinned footnote’s thumbtack always shows. When
                  there isn’t room, or one of your margin notes is already
                  beside that footnote, it sits on the footnote’s own line.</li>
                <li><strong>In that list, the Notebook’s “This verse” tab, and
                  the Study Desk’s Footnotes pane</strong>, it carries the word
                  “Pin” too.</li>
                <li><strong>In the popover a footnote letter opens</strong>, it
                  is the icon alone, and pointing at it, or reaching it with
                  Tab, shows its name.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Read View has no footnotes, so it has no pins.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="panel">
              <h3 id="panel">The Notebook panel</h3>
              <p>
                The Notebook sits in the margin beside the text on Scripture
                pages and in Read View. (A book’s introduction, an article, and
                the glossary have no Notebook.) It has four tabs:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>My Notes</strong> lists what you have kept. On a
                  Scripture page, choose “This chapter” or “Everything.” A long
                  note or pinned footnote opens with its caret, one at a
                  time.</li>
                <li><strong>This verse</strong> shows everything for the verse
                  you are on.</li>
                <li><strong>Greek</strong> opens <a href="#greek">the Greek
                  text</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>Settings</strong> holds the switches, the print
                  buttons, “My sheets,” and where your notebook is kept.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                The » button minimizes the panel to a tab at the window’s edge,
                and that tab brings it back. The “Open in Study Desk” button in
                the toolbar opens the <a href="#desk-page">Study Desk page</a>,
                where panes sit side by side. The panel’s words follow the text
                size and line spacing you choose in the Display settings (the
                “Aa” button), as the text beside it does.
              </p>

              <h4>Hide my marks</h4>
              <p>
                Your marks are your notes, highlights, and bookmarks. The key
                H, or the switch in Settings, hides them from the page, so you
                can read it clean, and puts them back when you press it again.
                Nothing is deleted.
              </p>

              <h4>Widths</h4>
              <p>
                On a Scripture page you can drag the edges between the text,
                your notes’ margin, and the Notebook to make each wider or
                narrower. If dragging is hard, click an edge instead (or press
                Enter on it): a small menu offers “Wider” and “Narrower,” a
                step at a time. The line between the Greek and a word’s entry
                works the same way. “Reset widths” in Settings puts them back.
              </p>
              <p>
                A small window, like Notebook actions, moves to the other side
                of the screen when you click the dots at the left of its title
                bar.
              </p>

              <h4>From the keyboard</h4>
              <p>
                Tabbing from the top of a Scripture page reaches “Skip to
                content” and then “Skip to the Notebook,” which opens the
                Notebook and moves into it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="print-part">
              <h3 id="print-part">Printing with your notes</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>Part of a chapter.</strong> On a Scripture page, a
                verse’s menu has a “Print ▸” choice under “My Notebook,” and so
                does the selection panel on Scripture pages and in Read View.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>In the verse menu it offers “This verse” (“These verses”
                  for a range) and, for the same verses, “with my notes” and
                  “with room to write.”</li>
                <li>For selected words it offers “This selection…” and the same
                  two.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                What prints is only those verses or words, headed with the
                reference, with the footnotes they cite and the LIT’s notice.
                “With my notes” leaves your notes and highlights on those
                verses beside the text; “with room to write” adds a ruled
                margin. “Hide my marks” changes only what is on the screen, so
                “with my notes” prints them either way. The choice appears only
                where the browser keeps a notebook.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>A whole chapter.</strong> Use your browser’s print, or
                the “Print…” choice on the Study Desk page or in the Notebook’s
                Settings tab.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 3. The Study Desk page ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="desk-page">
            <h2 id="desk-page">The Study Desk page</h2>
            <p>
              The <a href="/desk/">Study Desk page</a> sets up to {MAX_PANES}
              panes side by side, each a different tool, and each scrolls on
              its own.
            </p>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-open">
              <h3 id="desk-open">Opening it</h3>
              <ul>
                <li>The “Open in Study Desk” button in a Scripture page’s
                  toolbar (“Study Desk” where the toolbar is short) opens it at
                  that chapter. On a book’s introduction, the same button opens
                  it with the introduction in a Book Intro pane.</li>
                <li>The header’s “Study” menu, which also lists your sheets,
                  your notebook, and this help.</li>
                <li>The “Study Desk” button beside “Go to passage” on the
                  <a href="/read/">Read page</a>.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                These open the desk as you left it, and move only the LIT pane.
                A few links open it in its <a href="#desk-layout">Reading
                layout</a> instead: the search box’s second choice for a
                reference (“in Study Desk, Reading layout,” or Shift+Enter),
                the search page’s “Study Desk, Reading layout,” and the chapter
                picker’s “Open Romans in Study Desk →.”
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-panes">
              <h3 id="desk-panes">The kinds of pane</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>The LIT</strong> is a chapter of the translation,
                  with the verse menu and the selection tools of Scripture
                  pages. With more than one, the one you last worked in
                  leads.</li>
                <li><strong>My Notes</strong> lists what you have kept:
                  “Everything,” or “This chapter,” which is the leading LIT
                  pane’s chapter and changes as that pane moves.</li>
                <li><strong>This verse</strong> shows everything for one
                  verse.</li>
                <li><strong>Footnotes</strong> lists the chapter’s footnotes,
                  each with a way to pin it or add a note.</li>
                <li><strong>Book Intro</strong> is the introduction to the
                  book.</li>
                <li><strong>Greek</strong> is the chapter in Greek, the text
                  the LIT is translated from, as in the Notebook.</li>
                <li><strong>Sheets</strong> is where you make and edit
                  <a href="#sheets">sheets</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>Search</strong> searches the translation, the
                  glossary, the articles, the book introductions, and the
                  Greek (see <a href="#search-pane">The Search pane</a>).</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Each kind has a short description under its name in the menus.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-following">
              <h3 id="desk-following">Panes that follow, and “Stay here”</h3>
              <p>
                My Notes, This verse, Footnotes, Book Intro, and Greek follow
                the place of the leading LIT pane (Book Intro follows its
                book). A My Notes pane names the chapter it is on after its
                name.
              </p>
              <p>
                The lock in a pane’s header, “Stay here,” keeps that pane where
                it is while the others move on. Press it again and the pane
                follows again. While it is on, the lock is closed and tinted
                green, and in a wide pane the words “Stay here” sit beside it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-arrange">
              <h3 id="desk-arrange">Adding, moving, and sizing panes</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>The commands.</strong> A row under the page’s header
                holds “Add pane,” “Layout…,” “New sheet…,” “Print…,” and
                “Help.”
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>“Add pane”</strong> asks for a column (a
                  full-height pane at the right edge) or a row (a full-width
                  pane along the bottom), then for the kind. It is grayed out,
                  with the reason in its tooltip, when there are already
                  {MAX_PANES} panes or the window is too small to give a new
                  one room.</li>
                <li><strong>“New sheet…”</strong> starts a sheet at once: it
                  opens a Sheets pane (or uses the one you have) and asks which
                  template to begin from.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Each pane’s menu.</strong> Select a pane’s name to show
                something else in it, or to use:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>“Add pane”</strong> beside this one: left, right,
                  above, or below.</li>
                <li><strong>“Move”</strong> left, right, up, or down, swapping
                  places with the pane next to it.</li>
                <li><strong>“Size”</strong>: wider, narrower, taller, or
                  shorter.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>With the mouse.</strong> Drag a pane by its header:
                onto the edge of another to split it, onto its middle to swap
                the two. Drag the line between two panes to resize them. The ×
                in a pane’s header closes it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-full">
              <h3 id="desk-full">When the desk is full</h3>
              <p>
                Some things open a pane for you: a sheet sent to the desk (from
                the Sheets page, or a Search pane’s “New sheet of 3 verses”), a
                book introduction, a search result that needs a LIT or Greek
                pane, and Search from the / key. One rule covers all of them:
              </p>
              <ol>
                <li>A pane of that kind that is already open is used.</li>
                <li>Otherwise a new pane opens, if there is room.</li>
                <li>Otherwise, with {MAX_PANES} panes open or a window too
                  small for another, the new pane takes the place of the last
                  pane that isn’t a LIT pane. It never takes the pane you asked
                  from, or one set to “Stay here.”</li>
              </ol>
              <p>
                The bar at the bottom says what happened (“Book Intro shown in
                place of Greek.”), and its Undo puts the layout back. Where no
                pane can give up its place, a notice says why and offers both
                ways on: close a pane and try again, or open the thing on its
                own page. “Add pane” and “New sheet…” are commands, so on a
                full desk they are grayed out instead.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-layout">
              <h3 id="desk-layout">Layouts and Reading</h3>
              <p>
                “Layout…” offers ready-made arrangements. An arrangement that
                does not fit the window is grayed out, with the reason.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Reading</strong> is one LIT pane with verse numbers,
                footnote letters, key terms, and highlights turned off. Leaving
                it turns back on only what it turned off. It also sets the text
                like a page of a book, with both edges even and long words
                hyphenated at the ends of lines, in the default font and where
                the window is wide enough. “Show” is on its bar as in every
                layout, so any of the four can come back while you stay in
                Reading.
              </p>
              <p>
                “One pane” showing the LIT is not Reading: it leaves the text
                and its marks as you have them.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Your own default.</strong> The same window sets whether
                the page opens with your last layout or your own default, with
                “Make this my default” and “Reset to the original layout.” Your
                layout is kept in this browser.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-show">
              <h3 id="desk-show">What the text shows</h3>
              <p>
                “Show,” at the end of a LIT pane’s bar beside its passage,
                turns verse and chapter numbers, footnote letters, key terms,
                and your highlights on or off for that pane, in every layout.
                Each LIT pane keeps its own, so with two open, one can be plain
                text beside one with everything showing. They are kept with
                your layout, apart from the Scripture pages’ own settings.
              </p>
              <p>
                The font, theme, text size, and line spacing are for every
                pane, in “Display options,” in the title menu.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="desk-menus">
              <h3 id="desk-menus">Menus, keys, and printing</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>The title menu.</strong> “Study Desk ▾” in the page’s
                header opens a menu for the rest: “Display options,” “Full
                screen,” “My Notebook,” “Take the tour,” and the feedback link.
                “Full screen” uses the browser’s own full screen, and Escape
                leaves it.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Keys.</strong> With keyboard shortcuts on (“Display
                options”), the left and right arrows, or [ and ], go to the
                previous or next chapter in the leading LIT pane, as the arrows
                beside its passage do, and / opens a Search pane.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Print.</strong> “Print…” asks for a chapter, then
                offers to print it plainly, with your notes, or with room to
                write. Each choice opens the chapter in a new tab and prints
                there.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 4. Sheets ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="sheets">
            <h2 id="sheets">Sheets</h2>
            <p>
              A sheet gathers passages and your own words into a handout for a
              study, a class, or a sermon.
              <a href="/sheets/?sheet-tour">The Sheets tour</a> shows all of
              this in a minute, on the Sheets page or in the Study Desk.
            </p>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-make">
              <h3 id="sheet-make">Making a sheet</h3>
              <p>
                Make one in a Sheets pane with “New sheet,” or with “New
                sheet…” on the <a href="/sheets/">Sheets page</a>. Either
                starts from a template: Bible study, Sermon preparation, Class
                session, Lectio divina, Swedish method, Contextual Bible Study,
                Blank canvas, or one of your own. While the templates are
                showing, the button reads “Cancel.”
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Finding your sheets.</strong> They are listed on the
                Sheets page and in a Sheets pane. My Notebook lists them under
                their own heading, and the Notebook’s Settings tab links “My
                sheets.” In the list, “Duplicate” makes a copy of a sheet, and
                “Delete” moves it to the trash, like a note.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-parts">
              <h3 id="sheet-parts">Adding passages and parts</h3>
              <p><strong>A passage</strong> can come from four places:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>The sheet itself:</strong> “+ Passage,” then type a
                  reference.</li>
                <li><strong>A LIT pane:</strong> “Add to sheet” in the verse
                  menu, or beside a selection.</li>
                <li><strong>A Scripture page:</strong> “Add to sheet” in the
                  verse menu and a selection’s panel lists your latest sheets
                  and “New sheet,” and adds the passage without leaving the
                  page. The bar at the bottom then offers “Open the
                  sheet.”</li>
                <li><strong>A search:</strong> the verses you check (see
                  <a href="#search-sheet">Search results on a sheet</a>).</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                A passage always shows today’s wording of the LIT, and the
                sheet’s page tells you when the wording has changed since you
                added it.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>The other parts</strong> are “+ Text,” “+ Question,”
                “+ Space to write,” “+ Page break,” and “+ Key terms box.” The
                key terms box has “Move before the reading” or “Move after the
                reading,” so each sheet can have the group meet the words first
                or let the passage speak first.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>“+ From my notes…”</strong> lists your notes,
                highlights, and pinned footnotes on the sheet’s passages
                (everything, until it has a passage) to bring on as parts. A
                question note becomes a Question part, any other note or a
                pinned footnote becomes a Text part, and a highlight becomes a
                passage.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Adding in the middle.</strong> Point at the space
                between two parts and an “Add” pill appears on a line there.
                The pill adds a part at that spot, and its “Passage…” opens the
                passage form right under the part above, so a long sheet
                needn’t be scrolled to its end. From the keyboard, the pills
                are one stop in the Tab order: tab to one, then use the up and
                down arrows (or Home and End) to move between the gaps.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Moving a part.</strong> Drag it by its header, or use
                Move up and Move down. The formatting toolbar appears under the
                part you are writing in, and only there.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-templates">
              <h3 id="sheet-templates">Templates</h3>
              <p>
                A sheet started from one of the built-in templates opens with a
                short description above its parts: who the template is for and
                how a session with it moves. It never prints, and it goes away
                when you close it with its × (for that sheet) or once you add,
                remove, move, or rename a part.
              </p>
              <p>
                A template’s parts start empty. The dimmed words in each say
                what goes there, and they never print, copy, or appear when you
                present.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>A template’s headings print as they are, as cues, even with
                  nothing typed under them.</li>
                <li>A question, a key terms box, or a Leader only part left
                  empty prints nothing, so a question you want lines under
                  needs its words (or use a space to write).</li>
                <li>A passage you add goes under the template’s reading, or
                  after the last passage on the sheet.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Your own templates.</strong> “Save as template” keeps a
                sheet’s parts, without its passages, as a template of your own
                (a sheet marked “This is for a commercial project” passes that
                on). It is listed under “My templates,” where New sheet offers
                it and where you can open it to change it or delete it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-blanks">
              <h3 id="sheet-blanks">Fill-in-the-blank passages</h3>
              <p>
                In a passage, click a key term under “Fill in the blank,” or
                select words and choose “Blank,” to leave a gap for
                participants to fill in.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Hints.</strong> Each blank can carry a hint beneath it:
                none, the traditional word, or your own.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>For a key term, the traditional word is the usual one for
                  the Greek word behind it, in its dictionary form:
                  “traditionally believe” beneath “trusted.” It is not checked
                  against any one Bible, so where yours words the verse
                  differently, choose “My own hint…” and change the word it
                  starts from.</li>
                <li>Where the verse leaves more than one word possible, the
                  blank has no hint until you pick one from its menu.</li>
                <li>Where the LIT keeps the traditional word itself
                  (“faithful,” “demon”), a new blank starts with no hint, since
                  the hint would be the answer.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Footnotes.</strong> A footnote that would give a blank
                away, by using its words or by naming a traditional word the
                hint does not show, is held back until you decide whether it
                prints.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>To remove a blank</strong>, select its words in the
                passage and choose “Unblank,” or press Enter on them with the
                keyboard, or choose the × beside it in the list, which puts the
                words back.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-size">
              <h3 id="sheet-size">Text size</h3>
              <p>
                Under a sheet’s subtitle, “Text size” sets how big the whole
                sheet prints, from 11 points to 18 (large print). It sizes the
                sheet’s title, passages, and text in the editor and on paper,
                and Copy and Save as use it too. It is saved with the sheet, so
                a copy or a shared link keeps it.
              </p>
              <p>
                The text size in the Display settings sizes the rest of the
                Sheets page (its lists, choices, buttons, and labels), never
                the sheet.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-tabs">
              <h3 id="sheet-tabs">Saving, undoing, and two tabs</h3>
              <p>
                Every change is saved as you make it, and Ctrl+Z (⌘Z on a Mac)
                takes it back.
              </p>
              <p>
                A sheet open in two tabs stays the same in both: a change in
                one shows in the other. Opening a sheet to edit when another
                tab is already editing it asks first, “Continue here?” If both
                tabs change the same part, the one you are typing in keeps your
                words and says “Changed in another tab,” with “Show that
                version” and “Keep mine.”
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-versions">
              <h3 id="sheet-versions">Leader and participant versions</h3>
              <p>
                “Print or share” and “Present” in a Sheets pane open the
                sheet’s own page in a new tab (the small arrow out of a box
                after a label means that). On the sheet’s page you choose a
                version, “Leader version” or “Participant version.”
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>The participant’s leaves out the answers and the parts
                  marked “Leader only.”</li>
                <li>If a blank’s words also print elsewhere on the
                  participant’s version, a quiet note above the paper says
                  where.</li>
                <li>On the participant’s version, “Add answer key” adds a last
                  page with each blank’s answer by verse, to hand out once the
                  blanks are filled in.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Going back to editing.</strong> “Edit” on the sheet’s
                page changes the sheet there, and “Close” brings you back.
                “Edit in Study Desk” opens it in a Sheets pane, with the LIT
                beside it. A Sheets pane’s “Open on its own page” edits it on
                the Sheets page in a new tab.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sheet-output">
              <h3 id="sheet-output">Print, copy, save, present, share</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Print</strong> prints the version on screen, on
                  Letter or A4 paper.</li>
                <li><strong>Copy</strong> copies it, with its headings and
                  italics, to paste into Word, Google Docs, or Pages.</li>
                <li><strong>Save as</strong> saves it as a file: a Word
                  document (.docx), an OpenDocument text file (.odt), or a Rich
                  text file (.rtf). Under “Slides” it saves the screens of
                  Present as a PowerPoint file (.pptx) or an OpenDocument
                  slides file (.odp). A PDF comes from “PDF…,” which opens
                  Print, where you choose “Save as PDF.”</li>
                <li><strong>Present</strong> shows the sheet to a room, full
                  screen, one part at a time, with passages you can uncover a
                  blank at a time.
                  <ul>
                    <li>“Presenter view” opens a second window for you, with
                      the leader’s notes, that stays in step with the room’s
                      screen.</li>
                    <li>Press B (or a period) in either window to turn the
                      room’s screen to black while you talk, and again to bring
                      it back just as it was. An arrow or Space also brings it
                      back, without moving on.</li>
                    <li>If you give a part minutes, the presenter view counts
                      down the time left in it (and, quietly, the time over),
                      beside the sheet’s planned total and how long you have
                      been going.</li>
                  </ul>
                </li>
                <li><strong>Share…</strong> sends the sheet to someone as a
                  link or as a sheet file (.md).
                  <ul>
                    <li>It starts on the participant’s version, which leaves
                      out the Leader only parts and your answers to
                      questions.</li>
                    <li>Check “Include the answer key” to send the key as its
                      last page, or choose the leader’s version to send
                      everything. A line in the dialog says what is being
                      sent.</li>
                    <li>The person opens it on the Sheets page, and “Import
                      into my notebook” keeps a copy. The blanks’ answers are
                      words of the LIT, so someone who imports the sheet can
                      add the key themselves.</li>
                  </ul>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="sharing">
              <h3 id="sharing">Sharing what you write</h3>
              <p>
                What you write is yours. If you share it with the LIT’s words
                quoted inside, such as in a study guide or a bulletin, the
                <a href="/read/#license-terms">license terms</a> say how the
                translation may be quoted.
              </p>
              <p>
                A sheet’s License card has a box, “This is for a commercial
                project,” that checks the sheet against the commercial terms:
                how much of it is the LIT’s words, whether it quotes a whole
                book, whether every footnote prints, and whether it leaves
                blanks. Once it is checked, a sheet that goes beyond those
                terms asks before you print, copy, save, share, or present it
                from its page, and you can go ahead anyway.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 5. The Greek ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="greek">
            <h2 id="greek">The Greek</h2>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="greek-text">
              <h3 id="greek-text">Reading the Greek</h3>
              <p>
                On a Scripture page, the Notebook’s Greek tab shows the chapter
                in the Greek of the SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT), in its
                own paragraphs. It opens at the verse you are reading, and a
                “Go to verse” button brings you back to it. The Study Desk
                page’s Greek pane is the same thing. “This verse” also shows
                the Greek of the verse you are on. Like the rest of the
                notebook, it is for computers.
              </p>
              <p>
                A text-critical sign in the Greek opens the apparatus at the
                end of the text, which compares Westcott and Hort, Tregelles,
                NA28, and Robinson–Pierpont.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="greek-word">
              <h3 id="greek-word">A word’s details</h3>
              <p>
                Select a word, or move through the words with the arrow keys,
                to see its details at the foot of the tab. They show:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>its dictionary form, its parsing in words, and its Strong’s
                  number;</li>
                <li>an entry from a lexicon (see
                  <a href="#greek-sources">Lexicons and sources</a>);</li>
                <li>the LIT’s footnote letters for that verse, which open “This
                  verse”;</li>
                <li>for a word the glossary covers, how the LIT renders it in
                  that verse.</li>
              </ul>
              <p><strong>From the keyboard:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Left and Right step one word, Up and Down jump a verse, and
                  Home and End go to the first and last word of the
                  chapter.</li>
                <li>The words are one stop for Tab, so a keyboard reader passes
                  the whole text in one press.</li>
                <li>While a word is open, the line between the Greek and its
                  entry can be moved with the Up and Down keys (Shift for
                  larger steps), and Enter puts it back.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="greek-search">
              <h3 id="greek-search">Searching the Greek</h3>
              <p>To search the Greek, use the site’s search box.</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>A Greek word</strong>, with or without accents,
                  finds every form of it. So does a Strong’s number such as
                  G4102. The site’s search then shows the Greek alone.</li>
                <li><strong>A transliteration</strong> such as pistis, or
                  <strong>an English word</strong>: choose “Greek” in the
                  search’s type menu. An English word finds the Greek the LIT
                  renders that way for the glossary’s terms, such as justness
                  or self-preservation, then the Greek words a lexicon glosses
                  that way, such as faith.</li>
                <li><strong>From a word’s details</strong>, to see where else
                  it occurs: select its form, its dictionary form, its Strong’s
                  number, or its parsing, and the results take the place of the
                  text until you choose “Back to the text.” A result in another
                  chapter opens that chapter with the Greek tab at the
                  word.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="greek-sources">
              <h3 id="greek-sources">Lexicons and sources</h3>
              <p>
                You can choose between the UBS Dictionary of New Testament
                Greek and STEPBible’s brief lexicon, which shows the full LSJ
                entry under its own. Your choice is remembered in this browser.
              </p>
              <p>
                A lexicon may give a different sense from the LIT. That is
                expected. The LIT’s footnotes give the reasons for its own
                choices.
              </p>
              <p>
                The Greek data is shared under the licenses of its sources.
                They are listed under “Sources and licenses” at the foot of the
                tab.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 6. Search and commands ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="search">
            <h2 id="search">Search and commands</h2>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="search-box">
              <h3 id="search-box">The search box</h3>
              <p>
                With the Study Desk on, the site’s search box also looks in
                your notebook. A reference gives two choices: the Scripture
                page, or the Study Desk in its Reading layout. A book’s name
                with “intro” or “introduction” (“Matthew intro”) goes to that
                book’s introduction.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="search-pane">
              <h3 id="search-pane">The Search pane</h3>
              <p>
                On the Study Desk page, a Search pane searches the translation,
                the glossary, the articles, the book introductions, and the
                Greek. It stands alone and keeps nothing about what you
                searched for.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>A verse you choose moves the leading LIT pane, or opens one
                  if there is none.</li>
                <li>A book introduction, or a search such as “Matthew intro,”
                  opens in a Book Intro pane.</li>
                <li>A Greek result shows the word in a Greek pane.</li>
                <li>A passage result also has a link that opens the passage on
                  its own page, in a new tab.</li>
                <li>The pane’s link to the search page reads “Open the search
                  page” until you search, and “See results on the search page”
                  after.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                If the desk is full, a pane it opens follows
                <a href="#desk-full">the rule for a full desk</a>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="search-sheet">
              <h3 id="search-sheet">Search results on a sheet</h3>
              <p>
                Each verse a search finds has a checkbox. Only the verses you
                check go to a sheet. With none checked the buttons are grayed
                out, and pressing one says how to check. The box at the head of
                the list checks every verse. On the search page, chapters found
                by topic have no boxes, since each chapter goes whole.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>On the search page</strong>, the button carries the
                  count: “Add 4 verses to a sheet…” It asks which sheet, or a
                  new one, and then offers “Open the sheet” on the Sheets page,
                  with the Study Desk beside it.</li>
                <li><strong>In the Search pane</strong>, “New sheet of 3
                  verses” opens the sheet in a Sheets pane. While a Sheets pane
                  is showing a sheet, “Add 3 verses to” that sheet leads, and
                  the + on a verse adds that one verse.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="commands">
              <h3 id="commands">Commands</h3>
              <p>
                The search box understands a few commands. On a Scripture page
                or in Read View, for example, type “&gt;tour” and press Enter
                to take the tour again; other pages don’t offer it.
              </p>
              <p>
                “Notebook actions,” a link in the Notebook’s Settings tab, in
                the search tray, and beside the field on the search page, lists
                the commands the box understands on the page you are on. A row
                in it runs its command.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 7. Keeping your notebook ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="keeping">
            <h2 id="keeping">Keeping your notebook</h2>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="ways-to-keep">
              <h3 id="ways-to-keep">Where your notebook is kept</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>In this browser.</strong> Your notebook is kept in this
                browser, on this computer. Nothing is sent to the LIT Bible
                team or anywhere else.
              </p>
              <p><strong>What can clear it:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Clearing your browser’s data for this site.</li>
                <li>Closing a private window.</li>
                <li>A browser set to clear data when it quits.</li>
                <li>A different browser or computer, which each start with an
                  empty notebook.</li>
                <li>Your browser, on its own, when it is short of room.</li>
                <li><strong>Safari</strong>, which can clear a site’s stored
                  data after seven days of using Safari without visiting the
                  site. If you use Safari, save a copy now and then.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>The safe way.</strong> A copy is a file on your own
                computer, and it is the safe way to keep your notebook. When
                you first use the notebook you can ask to be reminded: the
                panel then tells you when it has been a week since you saved
                one. You can change that in the Notebook’s Settings tab.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Other ways.</strong> Other ways to keep it, across your
                computers and the apps, are being planned. Nothing here depends
                on them.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="copies">
              <h3 id="copies">Saving and opening a copy</h3>
              <p>
                <a href="/notebook/#backup">My Notebook</a> can save a copy in
                three kinds of file:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>A full backup</strong> (JSON), which holds
                  everything and is the one to open again here.</li>
                <li><strong>A Markdown file</strong>, to read, print, or move
                  into other writing.</li>
                <li><strong>A Web Annotation file</strong> (JSON), a standard
                  format other annotation tools can read.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                “Open a copy” on the same page puts a saved full backup back
                into the notebook.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section class="nbhelp__topic" aria-labelledby="delete-everything">
              <h3 id="delete-everything">The trash, and deleting everything</h3>
              <p>
                <strong>The trash.</strong> Deleting a note, or removing a
                bookmark or highlight, moves it to the trash, where it waits
                for {TRASH_DAYS} days.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>Undo (or Ctrl+Z, ⌘Z on a Mac) puts it back right away.</li>
                <li>After that, open the Trash in
                  <a href="/notebook/">My Notebook</a> and press Restore, on
                  one item or on all of them.</li>
                <li>After {TRASH_DAYS} days only a record that it was deleted
                  remains, so a saved copy is the only way back.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Deleting everything.</strong> To clear this browser, My
                Notebook has:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>“Delete all notes and marks”: every note, highlight,
                  bookmark, pinned footnote, and label.</li>
                <li>“Delete all sheets”: every sheet, and your own
                  templates.</li>
                <li>“Empty the trash now.”</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                The first two each leave the other alone. Clearing this site’s
                data in your browser also removes it all. Save a copy first if
                you may want it back.
              </p>
            </section>

            <p class="nbhelp__back"><a href="#contents">Back to contents</a></p>
          </section>

          {/* ============ 8. Send feedback ============ */}
          <section class="nbhelp__part" aria-labelledby="missing">
            <h2 id="missing">Send feedback</h2>
            <p>
              The Study Desk is in {DESK_STAGE}, and the site deliberately
              doesn’t measure who uses what, so we only hear what you tell us.
              If something is broken, confusing, or missing,
              <a href={FEEDBACK_HREF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{FEEDBACK_LABEL.toLowerCase()}</a>
              (it opens the contact form in a new tab). The same link is in the
              Study Desk menu on the desk page and at the foot of the
              Notebook’s Settings tab.
